<article class="env-definition" id="75DB7F">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/75DB7F">Definition <span data-tag="75DB7F">5.5.6.1</span>.</a> Let $\calC $ be an $\infty $-category and $k \geq -1$ an integer. We will say that an object $C$ of $\calC $ is <em>$k$-truncated</em> if, for every object $D \in \calC $, the space $\bHom _{\calC }(D,C)$ is $k$-truncated. By convention, we will say that $C$ is <em>$(-2)$-truncated</em> if it is a final object of $\calC $. We will say that an object of $\calC $ is <em>discrete</em> if it is $0$-truncated. We will generally let $\tau _{\leq k} \calC $ denote the full subcategory of $\calC $ spanned by the $k$-truncated objects.truncated!object of an $\infty $-category$k$-truncated!object of an $\infty $-categorydiscrete </p>
